// An electric guitar: six steel strings a pick sets moving, a pickup under them, and an
// amplifier and its speaker, which are half the instrument.
//
// Each string is a loop: a wave goes round it once a period, losing a little each time, its
// highs first (Karplus and Strong, "Digital synthesis of plucked-string and drum timbres", and
// Jaffe and Smith, "Extensions of the Karplus-Strong plucked-string algorithm", both Computer
// Music Journal 7(2), 1983; as one loop with its losses in one filter, Karjalainen, Välimäki
// and Tolonen, "Plucked-string models: from the Karplus-Strong algorithm to digital waveguides
// and beyond", CMJ 22(3), 1998). What goes round is the string's speed, which is what a pickup
// hears. (engine::bowed's string is two lines either side of a bow that holds it; nothing
// holds this one, so it is one.)
//
// What it models:
// - the pick: it pulls the string aside at a place and lets it go. Let go, the string's speed
//   at any point is a pulse as wide as the pick's distance from the bridge, there and back, so
//   a pick near the bridge is bright and one nearer the neck round. The pulse's edges are as
//   sharp as the pick is hard and the stroke fast. A string picked while it rings is stopped by
//   the pick first; down strokes and up strokes pull opposite ways;
// - the string: how long it rings (longer low than high), and how long its top does (new
//   strings ring bright for longer). A note picked hard starts a few cents sharp and settles,
//   as a string pulled far aside is stretched (Tolonen, Välimäki and Karjalainen, "Modeling of
//   tension modulation nonlinearity in plucked strings", IEEE Trans. Speech and Audio
//   Processing 8(3), 2000). Steel is stiff, so a string's partials are a little sharp of
//   harmonics, the more the higher (`stiff_for`), and a string moves two ways at once, toward
//   the pickup and across it, a cent or two apart. So the wave is never the same twice: its
//   partials drift against each other, and its two ways beat;
// - the pick's scrape as it leaves the string: a few milliseconds of noise at a note's start;
//   and a stopped string's slipping under its finger and on its fret, a little noise that
//   comes and goes with the string's own motion (`Guitar::sizzle`);
// - the hands: a finger let go stops its string; the picking hand's heel on the strings at
//   the bridge mutes them (a thud that dies in half a second, its top much sooner); a finger hammered on or
//   pulled off changes the string's length without the pick; a bend or a slide takes the pitch
//   there over a time; a held note can be swayed (a bend's vibrato only ever goes sharp),
//   wider the longer it is held; a note can be slid into from a fret under. A fingertip is
//   softer than the nut and takes a stopped note's top, the sooner the higher the note, so a
//   note up the neck is bright as it is picked and round after (`Guitar::finger`). Fingers
//   are heard: a scratch as one leaves a string, a rub as one slides, and one is a moment late
//   leaving its string for the next note's (`Guitar::fingers`, `Hand::late`). No
//   hand does anything twice the same way: each stroke's place and angle, where each finger
//   lands, each string's tuning, the fingers' press and each sway's depth, wait and rate are
//   all a little off, each its own way (`Guitar::loose`);
// - the pickup: it hears the string at its own place, and a wave passes that place twice, once
//   each way, upside down the second time. So harmonics with a node there are missing: at the
//   neck the sound is hollow and round, at the bridge thin and bright (Tillman, "Response
//   Effects of Guitar Pickup Position and Width", till.com, 2000; Paiva, Pakarinen and
//   Välimäki, "Acoustics and modeling of pickups", J. Audio Eng. Soc. 60(10), 2012). The place
//   is a distance from the bridge, so the missing harmonics move as a finger shortens the
//   string. A pickup has width: it is heard at two places, a single coil's close together, a
//   humbucker's two coils further apart, which takes more of the top. Its coil and the cable
//   are a resonance, a peak of a few decibels at 3 to 5 kHz and nothing much above it;
// - the amplifier (`Amp`, its controls; `Amped`, it running): lows taken out first (so that
//   driven hard it stays tight, not woolly), two valves one after the other, each clipping
//   one side sooner than the other and the second upside down, then the tone controls and a
//   pair of power valves that clip both sides alike (Pakarinen and Yeh, "A review of digital
//   techniques for modeling vacuum-tube guitar amplifiers", CMJ 33(2), 2009). All six strings
//   go through it together, which is why a driven chord growls: the valves make tones at the
//   sums and differences of its notes. What makes it crunch and not only clip is what isn't
//   steady in it: a valve's knee can be a corner on the side where its grid draws current;
//   driven past it, the valve chokes for a moment (its rest is pushed down, and comes back
//   over a hundredth of a second), so a hard stroke sputters and a chord's beating shows; a
//   small sound is partly missed where one power valve hands over to the other; a loud sound
//   pulls their supply down, so a note's start is louder than what follows; and it hisses. A
//   gate before it can shut all that out between notes. The amplifiers below are the same
//   one set differently: clean, crunch, lead, and five kinds of rock and metal;
// - the speaker: a 12-inch cone in a box passes 90 Hz to 5 kHz, with a bump at each end, and
//   from 900 Hz up unevenly: a cone there has resonances of its own, here 16 of them, each a
//   peak or a hollow of a few decibels. It is what makes a driven amplifier bearable: without
//   it the clipping's top is a fizz.
//
// The first version had none of the stiffness, the second way of moving, the scrape, the
// looseness, the sag or the cone's resonances. Its
// clean sound was heard as a guitar; its lead as a synthesizer, and its crunch as a poor
// plug-in. Driven hard, an exact string's wave is one pulse the same every period, held at
// one level: a synthesizer's pulse wave. A held driven A4's harmonics moved 0.1 to 0.3 dB
// from steady, and a note's first 30 ms were no louder than the rest of it
// (`a_driven_note_isnt_a_frozen_wave`).
//
// Clipping makes harmonics far past what 48,000 samples a second can hold, and those come back
// as tones that belong to no note. So the valves run at four times the rate: the sound is
// brought up through two half-band filters, clipped, and brought back down through two more
// (`Double`, `Halve`). A 1245 Hz tone through `LEAD_AMP` has its strongest stray tone 34 dB
// under the note with the valves at the audio rate, and 76 dB under at four times it
// (`the_valves_leave_no_tones_that_arent_the_notes`). Through `DEATH_METAL`, whose knee is a
// corner and whose drive is the most there is, it is 50 dB under at four times the rate.
//
// A player picks it (`Picking` notes, which `pickings` makes from a performance): a chord is
// strummed, each string a moment after the one before, down on the beat and up off it; each
// note goes to the string it lies lowest on that is free; a slurred note is hammered on or
// pulled off, or bent if it is a step away and the hand bends (as a finger pushes a string:
// slowly at first, quickest in the middle); a note marked staccato is palm-muted. No strum's
// strings are evenly apart or evenly hard.
//
// No note is measured from a recording yet: the strings' times, the pick, the pickups'
// resonances, the amplifier's corners and the speaker's are set from how the instrument is
// built, for a fit against recorded notes to settle. Not modelled: a string ringing in
// sympathy with another, fret noise, the amplifier's hum and hiss, and feedback from the
// speaker to the strings.
//
// Everything on the audio thread is f32 arithmetic in a fixed order, so both hosts give the
// same samples.

use perform::Performance
use score::{NO_SLUR, STACCATO, Score, TICKS}
use std::audio::SAMPLE_RATE
use std::collections::Vec
use std::math::{PI, TAU}
use tone::{Band, Noise, Svf, band, gain_of, key_hz, one_pole, scatter, seeded}

/// The audio thread's rate.
const RATE: f32 = f32(SAMPLE_RATE)

/// A guitar's strings.
pub const STRINGS: u32 = 6

const NONE: u32 = 4294967295

/// Each string's memory (samples, a power of two): a string as low as 24 Hz fits, a bass
/// guitar's lowest tuned down.
const SPAN: u32 = 2048

/// A Stratocaster's pickups are 6.375, 3.875 and 1.625 inches from the bridge of a string of
/// 25.5 inches, and a single coil hears about an inch of string, a humbucker 2.5 (Tillman).
/// Heard at two places, a pickup's first missing band is where a width twice their distance
/// apart would put it: half an inch apart for a single coil, an inch and a quarter for a
/// humbucker.
const NECK: f32 = 0.25
const BRIDGE: f32 = 0.0637
const SINGLE: f32 = 0.0196
const HUMBUCKER_WIDE: f32 = 0.049

/// How stiff the open strings of a common set are (0.046 to 0.010 inches, the lowest three
/// wound on cores of 0.018, 0.016 and 0.014 inches, on a 25.5 inch scale): each from its
/// steel, its thickness, its length and its pitch, `π³ E d⁴ / (64 T L²)` (Fletcher and
/// Rossing, ch. 2), with a wound string's stiffness its core's. Computed, not measured.
const STIFF: [f32; 6] = [0.00013, 0.00007, 0.000045, 0.00011, 0.00004, 0.000013]

/// An amplifier and its speaker: what each of its controls is set to. Every number is one a
/// player would turn: start from the nearest of the amplifiers below and change what is wrong.
pub struct Amp: Copy {
    /// How hard the valves are driven (dB; the hardest stroke's swing reaches about 1 before
    /// them). Under 0 it is clean; 15 to 25 crunches; from 35 a single note sings for seconds.
    pub drive: f32 = -6.0,
    /// The corner under which the lows are taken out before the valves (Hz). Low (50), the
    /// lows are clipped with the rest: thick, woolly, a fuzz. High (300), only the middle is
    /// clipped: tight, a low string's chug stays a punch.
    pub tight: f32 = 70.0,
    /// The valves: how much sooner each clips one side than the other (0: both alike, as a
    /// fuzz's diodes do), and how sharp the knee is where it clips (0: round, smooth; 1: a
    /// corner, gritty).
    pub lean: f32 = 0.2,
    pub edge: f32 = 0.0,
    /// How much a valve driven past its knee chokes on it (0: not at all): its rest is
    /// pushed down and takes a hundredth of a second to come back, so a hard stroke's start
    /// sputters and a chord's beating shows as grit. Over a half it spits and gates.
    pub spit: f32 = 0.0,
    /// The tone controls, after the valves (each a gain: 1 leaves its band as it is), and
    /// where the middle is (Hz): the bass is what is under two fifths of that, the treble
    /// what is over two and a half times it.
    pub bass: f32 = 1.0,
    pub mid: f32 = 1.0,
    pub treble: f32 = 1.0,
    pub mid_hz: f32 = 630.0,
    /// The power valves: how hard they are driven; how far their supply sags under a loud
    /// sound (0: not at all), which makes a note's start louder than what follows and the
    /// amplifier spongy; and how much of a small sound is missed where one valve hands over
    /// to the other (0: none), a buzz in a note's tail.
    pub power: f32 = 1.0,
    pub sag: f32 = 0.5,
    pub cross: f32 = 0.0,
    /// The hiss the amplifier hears with the pickup (dB of the hardest stroke's swing), and
    /// the level under which a gate before the amplifier shuts (dB; under -150 there is no
    /// gate): a gate cuts a muted note's tail and the hiss between notes.
    pub hiss: f32 = -96.0,
    pub gate: f32 = -200.0,
    /// The speaker: the corner under which it gives nothing, with its bump there (Hz); the
    /// cone's own peak (Hz, and how much of it is added); and the corner over which it gives
    /// nothing (Hz).
    pub cab_low: f32 = 90.0,
    pub cab_peak_hz: f32 = 2600.0,
    pub cab_peak: f32 = 0.5,
    pub cab_high: f32 = 4800.0,
    /// How uneven the cone's own resonances make the speaker from 900 Hz up (0: smooth; 1: a
    /// peak may double what is there, a hollow halve it), and the seed they are scattered by
    /// (another number: another speaker), which the player's hand is seeded from too.
    pub cab_rough: f32 = 0.5,
    pub cone: u32 = 12,
    /// The level of the whole (dB).
    pub level: f32 = 0.0,
}

/// What makes an electric guitar's sound: the strings, the pick, the pickup, and the amplifier
/// and speaker it is played through.
pub struct Guitar: Copy {
    /// The open strings' keys (MIDI's numbering), lowest first, and how many frets each has.
    pub open: [f32; 6] = [40.0, 45.0, 50.0, 55.0, 59.0, 64.0],
    pub frets: u32 = 22,
    /// Seconds a note takes to fall 60 dB at E2 and at E6; between, evenly by the octave.
    pub ring_low: f32 = 12.0,
    pub ring_high: f32 = 4.0,
    /// Seconds what a note has near 3 kHz takes to fall 60 dB: new strings' is longer.
    pub shine: f32 = 1.2,
    /// A fingertip is softer than the nut, and takes some of a wave's top each time the wave
    /// comes back from it: the seconds a stopped note at 110 Hz keeps what it has near 3 kHz.
    /// A note an octave up meets the finger twice as often and keeps it half as long (and none
    /// keeps it longer than `shine`). So a note high on the neck is bright as it is picked and
    /// round a moment later, which is what a driven amplifier makes sing; with the top kept
    /// (as every note kept it at first), a high note stayed a sharp pulse and the amplifier
    /// made a buzz of it, heard as a synthesizer.
    pub finger: f32 = 1.2,
    /// How stiff each open string is: its `n`th partial is the root of `1 + stiff n²` sharp of
    /// a harmonic.
    pub stiff: [f32; 6] = STIFF,
    /// A string moves two ways, toward the pickup and across it, and its two ends hold it a
    /// little differently each way: the cents the second way is sharp of the first, and how
    /// much of it the pickup hears beside the first.
    pub planes: f32 = 1.6,
    pub across: f32 = 0.4,
    /// The pick: where it is along the open string from the bridge (a share of the string),
    /// how long its edge takes to let the string go (ms) at the softest stroke and at the
    /// hardest, and the power of a stroke's hardness the string's swing goes by.
    pub pick_at: f32 = 0.13,
    pub pick_soft: f32 = 0.45,
    pub pick_hard: f32 = 0.1,
    pub touch: f32 = 1.3,
    /// How loud the pick's scrape is as it leaves the string, beside the stroke's swing.
    pub scrape: f32 = 0.12,
    /// How loud the fingers are on the strings, beside the hardest stroke's swing: the scratch
    /// as a finger leaves a string it stopped, and the rub of one sliding along it (louder on
    /// the three wound strings). A driven amplifier makes much of them.
    pub fingers: f32 = 0.015,
    /// How much a stopped string slips under its finger and on its fret as it moves: noise in
    /// the string, as a share of the string's own motion (two and a half times as much on the
    /// three wound strings, whose windings catch).
    pub sizzle: f32 = 0.018,
    /// The cents the hardest stroke starts sharp, and the seconds that takes to settle.
    pub sharp: f32 = 8.0,
    pub settles: f32 = 0.25,
    /// A palm-muted note: the seconds it takes to fall 60 dB, and the share of a low E's
    /// period the hand's heel smooths the string's wave over each time round (more: duller).
    pub mute: f32 = 0.5,
    pub mute_over: f32 = 0.1,
    /// Seconds a string takes to fall 60 dB once its finger has let go.
    pub stop: f32 = 0.07,
    /// The pickup: where it is along the open string from the bridge and how wide (shares of
    /// the string), and its resonance (Hz, and how sharp).
    pub pickup_at: f32 = NECK,
    pub pickup_width: f32 = SINGLE,
    pub pickup_hz: f32 = 4200.0,
    pub pickup_q: f32 = 2.2,
    /// The amplifier and speaker it is played through.
    pub amp: Amp = Amp {},
    /// How far the tuning and the fingers are from exact (1: as a player's are; 0: exactly in
    /// tune, every note where it is asked).
    pub loose: f32 = 1.0,
}

// ---- the amplifiers ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// None is a copy of a maker's amplifier, and none was set by ear or against a recording: each
// is set from what players of the music say they turn, for an ear to correct. Each one's
// `level` puts the same riff at the same loudness.

/// Clean: round and glassy, breaking up only when a chord is hit hard.
pub const CLEAN_AMP: Amp = Amp {}

/// Turned up until chords growl, with grit in it: a knee with some corner, valves that choke
/// a little on a hard stroke, a buzz in the tails.
pub const CRUNCH_AMP: Amp = Amp {
    drive: 22.0,
    tight: 140.0,
    edge: 0.5,
    spit: 0.3,
    mid: 0.8,
    treble: 1.1,
    cross: 0.02,
    hiss: -84.0,
    cab_peak: 0.6,
    level: -12.0,
}

/// Driven hard, its lows cut before the valves, round at the knee: a single note sings for
/// seconds.
pub const LEAD_AMP: Amp = Amp {
    drive: 38.0,
    tight: 300.0,
    edge: 0.2,
    spit: 0.1,
    mid: 1.1,
    treble: 0.9,
    hiss: -84.0,
    cab_peak: 0.4,
    cab_high: 4300.0,
    level: -14.3,
}

/// Dad rock: an old amplifier turned most of the way up. Not much drive before the power
/// valves, which are pushed and sag; the middle forward; it cleans up when picked softly and
/// barks when hit.
pub const DAD_ROCK: Amp = Amp {
    drive: 15.0,
    tight: 110.0,
    edge: 0.4,
    spit: 0.2,
    bass: 0.9,
    mid: 1.25,
    treble: 1.0,
    mid_hz: 800.0,
    power: 1.6,
    sag: 0.9,
    cross: 0.03,
    hiss: -86.0,
    cab_peak: 0.5,
    cab_high: 5000.0,
    level: -12.0,
}

/// Hair metal: the same amplifier with more valves' worth of drive. Bright, the upper middle
/// biting, smooth at the knee so chords stay clear and a lead sustains.
pub const HAIR_METAL: Amp = Amp {
    drive: 30.0,
    tight: 180.0,
    edge: 0.3,
    spit: 0.15,
    bass: 0.9,
    mid: 1.0,
    treble: 1.4,
    mid_hz: 900.0,
    sag: 0.4,
    hiss: -86.0,
    cab_peak_hz: 3200.0,
    cab_peak: 0.8,
    cab_high: 5500.0,
    level: -14.7,
}

/// Stoner rock: a fuzz into a big amplifier. The lows go into the clipping with everything
/// else, both sides clip alike and hard, the valves choke and spit, the supply sags, and the
/// speaker is dark: thick, woolly and slow.
pub const STONER: Amp = Amp {
    drive: 40.0,
    tight: 45.0,
    lean: 0.05,
    edge: 0.7,
    spit: 0.6,
    bass: 1.5,
    mid: 1.3,
    treble: 0.5,
    mid_hz: 400.0,
    power: 1.3,
    sag: 1.2,
    hiss: -82.0,
    cab_low: 75.0,
    cab_peak: 0.2,
    cab_high: 3400.0,
    level: -13.5,
}

/// Death metal: all the drive there is, a hard knee, the middle scooped out after it, the
/// lows and the top turned up: a grind, with a gate to keep it shut between notes.
pub const DEATH_METAL: Amp = Amp {
    drive: 46.0,
    tight: 240.0,
    edge: 0.8,
    spit: 0.2,
    bass: 1.5,
    mid: 0.45,
    treble: 1.6,
    mid_hz: 550.0,
    sag: 0.2,
    hiss: -84.0,
    gate: -58.0,
    cab_peak_hz: 2400.0,
    cab_peak: 0.9,
    cab_high: 5200.0,
    level: -16.6,
}

/// Metalcore: as much drive, but the lows cut hard before the valves and a supply that
/// doesn't sag, so a muted low string is a punch and not a boom; the low middle scooped, the
/// upper middle forward, and a gate that shuts the moment the strings stop.
pub const METALCORE: Amp = Amp {
    drive: 44.0,
    tight: 380.0,
    edge: 0.5,
    spit: 0.05,
    bass: 1.2,
    mid: 0.7,
    treble: 1.25,
    mid_hz: 400.0,
    sag: 0.1,
    hiss: -86.0,
    gate: -52.0,
    cab_peak_hz: 2800.0,
    cab_peak: 0.7,
    level: -16.1,
}

/// A wall: stoner rock's weight, death metal's drive and knee, and metalcore's gate. The lows
/// are cut before the valves enough to keep a muted string a punch, and put back after them;
/// the middle is left in, so two of these a side fill a room and don't hollow it.
pub const WALL: Amp = Amp {
    drive: 45.0,
    tight: 150.0,
    lean: 0.1,
    edge: 0.75,
    spit: 0.35,
    bass: 1.6,
    mid: 0.9,
    treble: 1.25,
    mid_hz: 480.0,
    power: 1.2,
    sag: 0.6,
    hiss: -84.0,
    gate: -58.0,
    cab_low: 80.0,
    cab_peak_hz: 2500.0,
    cab_peak: 0.6,
    cab_high: 4600.0,
    level: -15.0,
}

/// A bass guitar's amplifier: driven until it growls a little, its speaker wide at the bottom
/// and shut above 2.8 kHz.
pub const BASS_AMP: Amp = Amp {
    drive: 14.0,
    tight: 30.0,
    edge: 0.3,
    spit: 0.1,
    bass: 1.4,
    mid: 1.0,
    treble: 0.8,
    mid_hz: 500.0,
    sag: 0.4,
    hiss: -90.0,
    cab_low: 42.0,
    cab_peak_hz: 1800.0,
    cab_peak: 0.3,
    cab_high: 2800.0,
    level: -12.0,
}

// ---- the guitars ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The neck's single coil, into the clean amplifier.
pub const CLEAN: Guitar = Guitar { amp: CLEAN_AMP }

/// The bridge's humbucker, the pick nearer the bridge: what the driven amplifiers are played
/// with. `Guitar { amp: STONER, ..HUMBUCKER }` is it through another amplifier.
pub const HUMBUCKER: Guitar = Guitar {
    pickup_at: BRIDGE,
    pickup_width: HUMBUCKER_WIDE,
    pickup_hz: 3000.0,
    pickup_q: 1.8,
    pick_at: 0.1,
}

/// The humbucker into the crunch: chords growl, and a palm-muted low string chugs.
pub const CRUNCH: Guitar = Guitar { amp: CRUNCH_AMP, ..HUMBUCKER }

/// The humbucker into the lead.
pub const LEAD: Guitar = Guitar { amp: LEAD_AMP, ..HUMBUCKER }

/// A bass guitar: the same instrument an octave down (its lowest four strings are a bass's
/// four), its strings thicker, so stiffer and longer ringing, its pickup further from the
/// bridge, through the bass amplifier. Stiffness: guessed at twice a guitar's, not computed.
pub const BASS: Guitar = Guitar {
    open: [28.0, 33.0, 38.0, 43.0, 47.0, 52.0],
    ring_low: 16.0,
    ring_high: 6.0,
    shine: 0.9,
    stiff: [0.00025, 0.00016, 0.00011, 0.00008, 0.00006, 0.00004],
    pick_at: 0.16,
    pickup_at: 0.17,
    pickup_width: HUMBUCKER_WIDE,
    pickup_hz: 2500.0,
    pickup_q: 1.2,
    amp: BASS_AMP,
}

/// `g` tuned `down` semitones lower, every string.
pub fn tuned_down(g: Guitar, down: f32) -> Guitar {
    var open = g.open
    for s in 0..STRINGS {
        open[s] = open[s] - down
    }
    Guitar { open, ..g }
}

// ---- the notes --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A note for the guitar: when it starts and ends (samples), its key (MIDI's numbering; a
/// fraction is a fraction of a semitone), the string it is on (0 the lowest), how hard it is
/// picked (0 to 1), whether the hand's heel mutes it, whether it is picked at all (if not, it
/// is hammered on or pulled off from the note before on its string), the seconds its pitch
/// takes to arrive from that note's (0: at once; a bend or a slide), the cents it is swayed
/// once it has been held a while (0: not), and the semitones under itself it is picked at
/// and slid up from (0: picked where it is).
pub struct Picking: Copy {
    pub from: u32,
    pub to: u32,
    pub key: f32,
    pub string: u32,
    pub hard: f32 = 0.6,
    pub muted: bool = false,
    pub picked: bool = true,
    pub glide: f32 = 0.0,
    pub sway: f32 = 0.0,
    pub scoop: f32 = 0.0,
}

/// A guitarist's habits.
pub struct Hand: Copy {
    /// Seconds from one string to the next in a strummed chord, and how uneven a strum is (1:
    /// each gap from six tenths of that to fourteen, each string up to 15% harder or softer
    /// than the chord; 0: every gap and every string alike).
    pub strum: f32 = 0.011,
    pub uneven: f32 = 1.0,
    /// A chord that starts an odd number of these ticks into its bar is strummed upward, from
    /// the highest string, and a little softer (0: every strum is down). An eighth note: down
    /// on the beat, up off it.
    pub up_every: u32 = 480,
    pub up_soft: f32 = 0.85,
    /// How many of a chord's strings an upward strum reaches, from the highest.
    pub up_strings: u32 = 4,
    /// Seconds a note that isn't muted is let ring past its written end, unless its string is
    /// wanted sooner (0: a finger lets go as the note ends).
    pub ring: f32 = 0.0,
    /// Whether a note marked staccato is palm-muted.
    pub mutes: bool = true,
    /// A note slurred from one at most this many semitones away is bent to (0: every slur is
    /// hammered on or pulled off). A whole step takes `bend_time` seconds, a half step seven
    /// tenths of that.
    pub bends: u32 = 0,
    pub bend_time: f32 = 0.17,
    /// The cents a note played alone is swayed, if it is held `sway_over` seconds or more.
    pub sway: f32 = 0.0,
    pub sway_over: f32 = 0.45,
    /// The semitones under itself a note is picked and slid up from, when it starts a phrase
    /// (nothing sounded for `breath` seconds before it; 0: never).
    pub scoop: f32 = 0.0,
    pub breath: f32 = 0.15,
    /// Seconds a finger is late leaving its string when the next note is on another: the two
    /// sound together a moment (from a third of this to nearly twice it).
    pub late: f32 = 0.0,
}

/// A hand that plays chords and riffs: no bends, no vibrato.
pub const RHYTHM: Hand = Hand {}

/// A hand that plays a line: a slur of a whole step or less is a bend, a long note sways, a
/// phrase's first note is slid into from a fret under, and a finger is a hundredth of a
/// second late leaving its string for the next.
pub const SOLO: Hand = Hand { bends: 2, sway: 45.0, scoop: 1.0, late: 0.012 }

/// The string `key` lies lowest on: the highest whose open key is at or under it.
pub fn string_for(g: Guitar, key: f32) -> u32 {
    var s = 0
    for i in 0..STRINGS {
        if g.open[i] <= key + 0.01 {
            s = i
        }
    }
    s
}

/// Whether `key` can be played on string `s`.
fn reaches(g: Guitar, s: u32, key: f32) -> bool {
    key + 0.01 >= g.open[s] && key <= g.open[s] + f32(g.frets) + 0.01
}

/// A string is free for a note if what it last played ends by this long after the note starts.
const OVERLAP: u32 = 1440

/// The string for a note at `key` from sample `from`: the one it lies lowest on, or, if that
/// one is still sounding another note (`busy`: when each string's last note ends), the next
/// lower one that reaches it; if none is free, the one it lies lowest on.
fn free_string(g: Guitar, key: f32, from: u32, busy: [u32; 6]) -> u32 {
    let best = string_for(g, key)
    for back in 0..best + 1 {
        let s = best - back
        if reaches(g, s, key) && busy[s] <= from + OVERLAP {
            return s
        }
    }
    best
}

/// The notes of voice `voice` in `p`, a performance of `score`, as a guitarist plays them on
/// `g`: each from its onset to its release, picked as hard as its velocity.
/// - Notes written to start together are a chord: each on its own string, strummed from the
///   lowest string at the chord's onset (or from the highest, `Hand::up_every`, and then only
///   its `Hand::up_strings` highest).
/// - A note under the same slur as the one before, where that one's string reaches it, isn't
///   picked: it is hammered on or pulled off, or bent to (`Hand::bends`).
/// - A note marked staccato is palm-muted (`Hand::mutes`): it is left to die under the hand
///   (`Guitar::mute`), however short it is played, unless its string is picked again first.
pub fn pickings(
    score: Score,
    p: Performance,
    voice: u32,
    g: Guitar = Guitar {},
    hand: Hand = Hand {},
) -> Vec<Picking> {
    var mine: Vec<u32> = Vec::new()
    for i in 0..p.notes.len() {
        if p.notes[i].voice == voice {
            mine.push(i)
        }
    }
    var out: Vec<Picking> = Vec::new()
    var busy = [0; 6]
    var last_slur = NO_SLUR
    var last_string = 0
    var last_alone = false
    // The note before, if it was played alone: where it is in `out`, and when it was let go
    // as written.
    var last_at = NONE
    var last_end = 0
    var at = 0
    loop {
        if at >= mine.len() {
            break
        }
        // The notes written to start with this one.
        let start = score.notes[p.notes[mine[at]].note].start
        var end = at + 1
        loop {
            if end >= mine.len() || score.notes[p.notes[mine[end]].note].start != start {
                break
            }
            end += 1
        }
        if end - at == 1 {
            let n = p.notes[mine[at]]
            let written = score.notes[n.note]
            let key = f32(n.key)
            let from = samples(n.onset)
            let to = max(samples(n.release), from + 1)
            let slurred = written.slur != NO_SLUR && written.slur == last_slur && last_alone
            var note = Picking {
                from,
                to,
                key,
                string: 0,
                hard: clamp(n.velocity, 0.0, 1.0),
                muted: hand.mutes && written.has(STACCATO),
            }
            if slurred && reaches(g, last_string, key) {
                let before = out[out.len() - 1]
                note.string = last_string
                note.picked = false
                note.muted = before.muted
                let apart = abs(key - before.key)
                // An open string can't be bent to, nor from.
                let stopped = key > g.open[last_string] + 0.5 &&
                    before.key > g.open[last_string] + 0.5
                if stopped && apart <= f32(hand.bends) + 0.01 {
                    note.glide = hand.bend_time * sqrt(max(apart, 0.25) / 2.0)
                }
            } else {
                note.string = free_string(g, key, from, busy)
                // Slid into, if it starts a phrase or is leaned on, and there is a fret under.
                let starts = last_at == NONE || from >= last_end + samples(f64(hand.breath))
                let room = key - hand.scoop > g.open[note.string] - 0.01
                if hand.scoop > 0.0 && room && !note.muted && starts {
                    note.scoop = hand.scoop
                }
                // The finger on the note before is late leaving, if that was another string.
                if last_at != NONE && out[last_at].string != note.string && !out[last_at].muted {
                    let lingers = hand.late * (0.3 + 1.4 * scatter(n.note, b: 3, c: 5))
                    if hand.late > 0.0 && out[last_at].to <= from + OVERLAP {
                        out[last_at].to = max(out[last_at].to, from + samples(f64(lingers)))
                    }
                }
            }
            let long = to - from >= samples(f64(hand.sway_over))
            if long && !note.muted && key > g.open[note.string] + 0.5 {
                note.sway = hand.sway
            }
            busy[note.string] = to
            note.to = if note.muted { from + samples(f64(g.mute)) } else {
                to + samples(f64(hand.ring))
            }
            last_string = note.string
            last_slur = written.slur
            last_alone = true
            last_at = out.len()
            last_end = to
            out.push(note)
        } else {
            // A chord, its notes from the lowest key up: each takes a string, and the strum
            // crosses them in the strings' order.
            var first = samples(p.notes[mine[at]].onset)
            for k in at..end {
                first = min(first, samples(p.notes[mine[k]].onset))
            }
            let begun = out.len()
            for k in at..end {
                let n = p.notes[mine[k]]
                let written = score.notes[n.note]
                let key = f32(n.key)
                let string = free_string(g, key, first, busy)
                // Every note of the chord holds its string from the strum's start.
                busy[string] = 4294967295
                out.push(
                    Picking {
                        from: first,
                        to: max(samples(n.release), first + 1),
                        key,
                        string,
                        hard: clamp(n.velocity, 0.0, 1.0),
                        muted: hand.mutes && written.has(STACCATO),
                    },
                )
            }
            let up = hand.up_every > 0 &&
                start % hand.up_every == 0 &&
                (start % score.meter.bar() / hand.up_every) % 2 == 1
            let apart = f32(samples(f64(hand.strum)))
            // Each note's place in the strum: how many of the chord's strings come before its.
            var places: Vec<u32> = Vec::new()
            for a in begun..out.len() {
                var before = 0
                for b in begun..out.len() {
                    let earlier = if up { out[b].string > out[a].string } else {
                        out[b].string < out[a].string
                    }
                    if earlier {
                        before += 1
                    }
                }
                places.push(before)
            }
            for a in begun..out.len() {
                // The gaps before it, none quite the usual, and how hard its string is hit.
                var after = 0.0
                for k in 0..places[a - begun] {
                    after += apart * (1.0 + hand.uneven * (0.8 * scatter(start, b: k, c: 7) - 0.4))
                }
                let hit = 1.0 +
                    hand.uneven * (0.3 * scatter(start, b: places[a - begun], c: 11) - 0.15)
                var note = out[a]
                note.from = first + u32(after + 0.5)
                note.to = max(note.to, note.from + 1)
                note.hard = clamp(note.hard * hit * (if up { hand.up_soft } else { 1.0 }), 0.0, 1.0)
                out[a] = note
            }
            for a in begun..out.len() {
                busy[out[a].string] = out[a].to
                out[a].to = if out[a].muted { out[a].from + samples(f64(g.mute)) } else {
                    out[a].to + samples(f64(hand.ring))
                }
            }
            // An upward strum reaches only its first strings.
            if up {
                var kept = begun
                for a in begun..out.len() {
                    if places[a - begun] < hand.up_strings {
                        out[kept] = out[a]
                        kept += 1
                    }
                }
                out.truncate(kept)
            }
            last_slur = NO_SLUR
            last_alone = false
            last_at = NONE
        }
        at = end
    }
    // By when each starts: a strum's strings, and a note that follows it closely.
    out.sort_by(|a, b| a.from < b.from)
    out
}

fn samples(seconds: f64) -> u32 {
    u32(max(seconds, 0.0) * f64(SAMPLE_RATE) + 0.5)
}

// ---- the amplifier ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A half-band filter's taps either side of its middle (which is a half), nearest first: the
/// rest are zero, so doubling or halving a rate through it costs few multiplies. Each is a
/// Kaiser-windowed sinc. The first is the pair between the audio rate and twice it: within
/// 0.001 dB to 9 kHz, and 79 dB down from 39 kHz, so that what folds back under 9 kHz is 79 dB
/// down (the speaker passes nothing over 5 kHz). The second is the pair between twice the rate
/// and four times it, where there is more room: 69 dB down from 87 kHz.
const NEAR: [f32; 5] = [0.304193017, -0.069794264, 0.018637952, -0.003130997, 0.000094291]
const FAR: [f32; 5] = [0.285025375, -0.035972276, 0.000946901, 0.0, 0.0]

/// A rate doubled: each sample in gives two out, the sample itself (as it was `reach` samples
/// ago) and the one halfway to the next.
struct Double: Copy {
    taps: [f32; 5],
    reach: u32,
    was: [f32; 10],
}

fn double(taps: [f32; 5], reach: u32) -> Double {
    Double { taps, reach, was: [0.0; 10] }
}

impl Double {
    @audio
    fn next(mut self, x: f32) -> (f32, f32) {
        for d in 0..9 {
            self.was[9 - d] = self.was[8 - d]
        }
        self.was[0] = x
        let k = self.reach
        var between = 0.0
        for j in 0..k {
            between += self.taps[j] * (self.was[k - 1 - j] + self.was[k + j])
        }
        (self.was[k], 2.0 * between)
    }
}

/// A rate halved: two samples in give one out, with what the lower rate can't hold taken out
/// first.
struct Halve: Copy {
    taps: [f32; 5],
    reach: u32,
    was: [f32; 20],
}

fn halve(taps: [f32; 5], reach: u32) -> Halve {
    Halve { taps, reach, was: [0.0; 20] }
}

impl Halve {
    @audio
    fn next(mut self, a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {
        for d in 0..18 {
            self.was[19 - d] = self.was[17 - d]
        }
        self.was[1] = a
        self.was[0] = b
        let middle = 2 * self.reach - 1
        var y = 0.5 * self.was[middle]
        for j in 0..self.reach {
            y += self.taps[j] * (self.was[middle - 1 - 2 * j] + self.was[middle + 1 + 2 * j])
        }
        y
    }
}

/// A valve: it clips softly, more softly on the side where it shuts off than on the side
/// where its grid starts to draw current, which is a knee as sharp as `edge` (0: as round as
/// the other side; 1: a corner).
@audio
fn valve(x: f32, edge: f32) -> f32 {
    if x > 0.0 {
        return (1.0 - edge) * tanh(x) + edge * min(x, 1.0)
    }
    tanh(x)
}

/// The valves run at this many times the audio rate.
const TIMES: f32 = 4.0
/// Between the valves: nothing under this passes (Hz: a coupling capacitor), and the top is
/// rounded from this corner (Hz), as a valve's own capacitance does.
const BLOCKS: f32 = 30.0
const ROUNDS: f32 = 6500.0
/// A valve driven past its knee draws current through its grid, which charges the capacitor
/// before it and pushes the valve's rest down, so it clips lopsidedly until the charge leaks
/// away (Pakarinen and Yeh call it blocking distortion). At `Amp::spit` of 1, what is over
/// the knee moves the rest by `CHARGES` of itself each sample, and the charge leaks away in
/// `LEAKS` seconds.
const CHARGES: f32 = 0.002
const LEAKS: f32 = 0.015
/// The supply's sag follows the power valves' sound over about a twentieth of a second (a
/// corner at 3 Hz).
const SAGS: f32 = 3.0
/// The gate hears the pickup's level come up in a millisecond and fall over a hundredth of a
/// second, and opens or shuts over two milliseconds. (Hearing the level fall over a twentieth
/// of a second, it took a quarter of a second to shut after a loud chord.)
const GATE_UP: f32 = 0.02
const GATE_DOWN: f32 = 0.002
const GATE_MOVES: f32 = 0.01
/// The cone's own resonances: how many, the lowest's frequency (Hz) and the octaves they
/// are spread over, one to each equal part of that, scattered within it. Each is 4 to 10
/// sharp, and adds or takes away (which, and how much of `Amp::cab_rough`, the seed
/// scatters too), so the speaker's answer from 900 Hz to 5 kHz is peaks and hollows a few
/// decibels either way, as a cone's is once it stops moving as one piece. Under 900 Hz there
/// are none: a body of resonances over the whole range (engine::bowed's `Body`, 96 of them)
/// was tried as the speaker, and its low ones took 20 ms to speak, so a low note's first 30 ms
/// were 3.7 dB under the rest of it.
const CONES: u32 = 16
const CONE_FROM: f32 = 900.0
const CONE_OCTAVES: f32 = 2.6

/// An amplifier and its speaker being played through: what the audio thread owns.
pub struct Amped {
    /// The hiss, and its level.
    noise: Noise,
    hiss: f32,
    /// The gate: the level under which it shuts (0: there is none), the pickup's level as it
    /// hears it, and how far open it is.
    gate_at: f32,
    heard: f32,
    open: f32,
    /// The lows taken out first: the corner's share, and what is under it.
    tight: f32,
    under: f32,
    up_near: Double,
    up_far: Double,
    down_far: Halve,
    down_near: Halve,
    /// Each valve's gain, its lean, its knee, and what the valve gives at rest.
    gain: f32,
    lean: f32,
    edge: f32,
    rest: f32,
    /// The grids: the share of what is over the knee that charges each sample, the share of
    /// the charge that leaks, and each valve's charge.
    charges: f32,
    leaks: f32,
    charge: [f32; 2],
    /// Between the valves and after them: the corners' shares, and what each holds.
    blocks: f32,
    rounds: f32,
    steady: f32,
    round: f32,
    steady2: f32,
    /// The tone controls: their corners' shares, what is under each, and the three gains.
    bass_at: f32,
    treble_at: f32,
    lows: f32,
    not_tops: f32,
    bass: f32,
    mid: f32,
    treble: f32,
    /// The power valves: how hard they are driven, and how much of a small sound they miss
    /// where one hands over to the other.
    power: f32,
    cross: f32,
    /// The supply: how far it sags, the share of the way its load is followed each sample,
    /// the load (the power valves' mean square), and what is left of the supply.
    sag: f32,
    sags: f32,
    load: f32,
    supply: f32,
    /// The speaker: its floor, its cone's peak and its top (two filters, together as steep as
    /// four poles and flat to the corner), and the cone's own resonances with what each adds.
    floor: Band,
    floor_state: Svf,
    peak: Band,
    peak_state: Svf,
    peak_gain: f32,
    top: [Band; 2],
    top_state: [Svf; 2],
    cone: [Band; 16],
    cone_state: [Svf; 16],
    cone_gain: [f32; 16],
    level: f32,
}

/// An amplifier set as `a` says, ready to play through.
pub fn amped(a: Amp) -> Amped {
    var scatter = seeded(a.cone)
    var cone = [Band {}; 16]
    var cone_gain = [0.0; 16]
    for m in 0..CONES {
        let along = (f32(m) + 0.5 + 0.4 * scatter.next()) / f32(CONES)
        cone[m] = band(hz: CONE_FROM * exp2(CONE_OCTAVES * along), q: 7.0 + 3.0 * scatter.next())
        let much = 0.3 + 0.7 * abs(scatter.next())
        cone_gain[m] = a.cab_rough * (if scatter.next() < 0.0 { -0.7 * much } else { much })
    }
    Amped {
        noise: seeded(a.cone + 31),
        hiss: gain_of(a.hiss),
        gate_at: if a.gate > -150.0 { gain_of(a.gate) } else { 0.0 },
        heard: 0.0,
        open: if a.gate > -150.0 { 0.0 } else { 1.0 },
        tight: one_pole(a.tight),
        under: 0.0,
        up_near: double(NEAR, reach: 5),
        up_far: double(FAR, reach: 3),
        down_far: halve(FAR, reach: 3),
        down_near: halve(NEAR, reach: 5),
        gain: gain_of(0.5 * a.drive),
        lean: a.lean,
        edge: a.edge,
        rest: valve(a.lean, a.edge),
        charges: CHARGES * a.spit,
        leaks: 1.0 / (LEAKS * TIMES * RATE),
        charge: [0.0; 2],
        blocks: one_pole(BLOCKS / TIMES),
        rounds: one_pole(ROUNDS / TIMES),
        steady: 0.0,
        round: 0.0,
        steady2: 0.0,
        bass_at: one_pole(a.mid_hz / 2.5 / TIMES),
        treble_at: one_pole(a.mid_hz * 2.5 / TIMES),
        lows: 0.0,
        not_tops: 0.0,
        bass: a.bass,
        mid: a.mid,
        treble: a.treble,
        power: a.power,
        cross: a.cross,
        sag: a.sag,
        sags: one_pole(SAGS / TIMES),
        load: 0.0,
        supply: 1.0,
        floor: band(hz: a.cab_low, q: 1.1),
        floor_state: Svf {},
        peak: band(hz: a.cab_peak_hz, q: 2.0),
        peak_state: Svf {},
        peak_gain: a.cab_peak,
        top: [band(hz: a.cab_high, q: 0.541), band(hz: a.cab_high, q: 1.307)],
        top_state: [Svf {}; 2],
        cone,
        cone_state: [Svf {}; 16],
        cone_gain,
        level: gain_of(a.level),
    }
}

impl Amped {
    /// One sample at four times the rate through the valves and the tone controls.
    @audio
    fn valves(mut self, x: f32) -> f32 {
        // The first valve, about a rest its grid's charge has pushed down.
        let into = self.gain * x + self.lean - self.charge[0]
        let a = valve(into, self.edge) - self.rest
        self.charge[0] += self.charges * max(into - 1.0, 0.0) - self.leaks * self.charge[0]
        // Between the valves nothing steady passes, and the top is rounded.
        self.steady += self.blocks * (a - self.steady)
        self.round += self.rounds * (a - self.steady - self.round)
        // The second valve is upside down, as each valve turns its signal over: it clips the
        // other side sooner.
        let next = -self.gain * self.round + self.lean - self.charge[1]
        let b = self.rest - valve(next, self.edge)
        self.charge[1] += self.charges * max(next - 1.0, 0.0) - self.leaks * self.charge[1]
        self.steady2 += self.blocks * (b - self.steady2)
        let c = b - self.steady2
        // The tone controls: what is under the bass's corner, what is over the treble's, and
        // the middle between.
        self.lows += self.bass_at * (c - self.lows)
        self.not_tops += self.treble_at * (c - self.not_tops)
        let toned = self.bass * self.lows +
            self.mid * (self.not_tops - self.lows) +
            self.treble * (c - self.not_tops)
        // The power valves: a pair, one pushing and one pulling, so both sides clip alike.
        // Where one hands over to the other a small sound is missed. What they can give is
        // what their supply is, and a loud sound pulls the supply down.
        var push = self.power * toned / self.supply
        if self.cross > 0.0 {
            push -= self.cross * tanh(push / self.cross)
        }
        let out = self.supply * tanh(push)
        self.load += self.sags * (out * out - self.load)
        self.supply = 1.0 / (1.0 + self.sag * self.load)
        out
    }

    /// What the valves make of `x` (a sample at the audio rate), before the speaker.
    @audio
    fn driven(mut self, x: f32) -> f32 {
        // The gate, and the hiss it shuts out with the rest.
        let level = abs(x)
        self.heard += (if level > self.heard { GATE_UP } else { GATE_DOWN }) * (level - self.heard)
        if self.gate_at > 0.0 {
            let wide = min(self.heard / self.gate_at, 1.0)
            self.open += GATE_MOVES * (wide * wide - self.open)
        }
        let heard = self.open * (x + self.hiss * self.noise.next())
        self.under += self.tight * (heard - self.under)
        let (a, b) = self.up_near.next(heard - self.under)
        let (a0, a1) = self.up_far.next(a)
        let (b0, b1) = self.up_far.next(b)
        let v0 = self.valves(a0)
        let v1 = self.valves(a1)
        let first = self.down_far.next(v0, v1)
        let v2 = self.valves(b0)
        let v3 = self.valves(b1)
        let second = self.down_far.next(v2, v3)
        self.down_near.next(first, second)
    }

    /// What the amplifier and its speaker make of `x`, the pickup's sample.
    @audio
    pub fn next(mut self, x: f32) -> f32 {
        let y = self.driven(x)
        let over = self.floor_state.high(self.floor, y)
        let peaked = over + self.peak_gain * self.peak_state.pass(self.peak, over)
        let once = self.top_state[0].low(self.top[0], peaked)
        let smooth = self.top_state[1].low(self.top[1], once)
        var rough = smooth
        for m in 0..CONES {
            rough += self.cone_gain[m] * self.cone_state[m].pass(self.cone[m], smooth)
        }
        self.level * rough
    }
}

// ---- the strings ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The planes a string moves in: toward the pickup, and across it.
const PLANES: u32 = 2
/// The all-pass filters in each plane's loop that make the string stiff.
const STIFFS: u32 = 4
/// The swing of the hardest stroke: the pickup's sound then reaches about 1.
const PICK: f32 = 0.5
/// What a string keeps of its ringing where the pick touches it for a new stroke.
const HUSH: f32 = 0.2
/// A finger hammered on or pulled off: the share of the way to its new length the string goes
/// each sample (there in 3 ms), and the swing it adds, beside a picked note's as hard.
const SNAP: f32 = 0.02
const TAP: f32 = 0.35
/// Where a ringing string's top is set (Hz).
const SHINE_HZ: f32 = 3000.0
/// The hand on a string, the picking hand's heel or a finger letting go, takes the wave's top
/// each time round: the wave comes back as its mean over the last `span` samples, which holds
/// every tone back the same time, so the note's harmonics stay in tune as they die. (A low-pass
/// strong enough to mute a low E, with one pole, held its fundamental back 60 samples and its
/// third harmonic 35: the harmonics rang 45 and 76 cents sharp, and a muted note read 22 cents
/// sharp.) A muted low E's span is `Guitar::mute_over` of its period; a higher note's is less
/// by the 3/2 power of its pitch, so that each note's first few harmonics die in the same
/// times. Each plane of each string remembers `PALM` samples for it.
const PALM: u32 = 256
const LOW_E: f32 = 82.41
/// The pick's scrape lasts this many samples (4 ms).
const SCRAPES: u32 = 192
/// A hand is never the same twice. Each stroke's place along the string is within this share
/// of the usual, either way, and its angle (how much of it sets the string moving across the
/// pickup rather than toward it) between `TILT` less `TILTS` and `TILT` more (radians). A
/// stopped note's pitch drifts `DRIFTS` cents (RMS), slowly, as the finger's press does, each
/// string's its own way; a finger's press stretches the string, so a stopped note is up to
/// `LANDS` cents sharp, each its own amount; a bent note lands within `BEND_LANDS` cents. And
/// no guitar is quite in tune: each string is within `TUNED` cents of its pitch. (At 1.5, 1.5
/// and 2 cents, chords high on the neck were still heard as nearer a synthesizer than the
/// low ones. At 2.5, 4 and 3 cents, with bends landing within 6, each sway its own depth by
/// four tenths and a finger 35 ms late off its string, a lead was heard as an amateur's: a
/// good player's bends land, their sway is even, and their fingers are quick and quiet. What
/// is loose now is what no hand can help.) So two strings' harmonics that would sit on each other beat slowly instead,
/// which is what moves in a driven chord.
const PLACES: f32 = 0.12
const TILT: f32 = 0.5
const TILTS: f32 = 0.25
const DRIFTS: f32 = 1.2
const LANDS: f32 = 2.5
const TUNED: f32 = 3.0
const BEND_LANDS: f32 = 2.5
/// A hand is never still: its own tremor presses and eases the strings, and every stopped
/// note's pitch trembles with it, `TREMBLES` cents (RMS), noise through a low-pass at
/// `TREMBLE_HZ`. It is the hand's, so the notes of a chord tremble together; each finger adds
/// `TREMBLE_OWN` of its own. And a string pushed across its fret by a bend, or slid along,
/// rubs: noise as loud as `Guitar::fingers` times `RUBS` when the pitch moves `RUB_AT` cents a
/// sample (a whole step's bend at its quickest), louder on the wound strings.
///
/// And a stopped string is never held quite fast: it slips a little under the fingertip and
/// on the fret as it moves, which is noise in the string as loud as `Guitar::sizzle` of the
/// string's own motion (so it is the note's: it comes and goes with it).
///
/// What the sizzle is for: without it a held driven note has 65 to 76 dB less between its
/// harmonics than at them, cleaner than anything a hand holds, and was heard as a
/// synthesizer. The tremble alone leaves it there. With noise in every stopped string that
/// was as loud whatever the string was doing (35 to 47 dB under the harmonics, and on the
/// wound strings 14 dB more than on the plain ones), the lead was heard as natural, but with
/// something apart from the note, and a chord's frequencies as pulled apart. And a rub under
/// every sway pulsed twice a sway and grew as the sway widened. A sway rubs nothing now, and
/// the noise is a share of the string's motion.
///
/// Set beside two recordings (docs/citations.md, the guitar's references), by how far a held
/// sound's spectral peaks stand over the median between them in three bands (150 to 500 Hz,
/// 500 Hz to 1.5 kHz, 1.5 to 4 kHz). A recorded solo's last note: 31, 40 and 26 dB; the lead's
/// A4 here with a sizzle of 0.012: 35, 42 and 39 dB. A recorded metal riff, mid-phrase: 9 to
/// 16, 18 to 20 and 12 to 14 dB; the death metal and metalcore amplifiers' riff here: 19 to
/// 22, 19 to 22 and 15 to 16 dB. So the model is still the cleaner, most of all at the top
/// of a single note and at the bottom of a riff, and the sizzle went up half as much again.
const TREMBLES: f32 = 0.55
const TREMBLE_OWN: f32 = 0.2
const TREMBLE_HZ: f32 = 12.0
const RUBS: f32 = 1.0
const RUB_AT: f32 = 0.04
/// (A bend was first the string's length going a share of the way to its new length each
/// sample: quickest at the start and slowing ever after, which is a synthesizer's glide, and
/// was heard as one.)
/// A sway: its usual rate (Hz), the seconds a note is held before it starts, and the seconds
/// it takes to come in. None of it is the same twice (engine::bowed found a vibrato at one
/// rate and one depth, coming in the same on every note, to be a synthesizer's): each note's
/// depth and wait are within `SWAY_OWN` of the usual, and through a note the depth wanders by
/// `SWAY_LOOSE` of itself and the rate by a quarter of that.
const SWAY_HZ: f32 = 5.8
const SWAY_AFTER: f32 = 0.22
const SWAY_IN: f32 = 0.35
const SWAY_OWN: f32 = 0.2
const SWAY_LOOSE: f32 = 0.12
/// A sway widens while its note is held: by this share of itself each second, for two seconds.
/// And its first swing is slow: it starts at `SWAY_SLOW` of its rate and is up to it in
/// `SWAY_QUICKENS` seconds. (A recorded solo's last note, held 1.2 s: straight for a quarter
/// of a second, a first swing of a third of a second, then swings of a sixth of a second, 85
/// to 140 cents from top to bottom. The sways here were under 50.)
const SWAY_GROWS: f32 = 0.3
const SWAY_SLOW: f32 = 0.6
const SWAY_QUICKENS: f32 = 0.35
/// A note slid into arrives in this many seconds; a finger leaving its string scratches for
/// `LIFTS` samples (10 ms).
const SCOOPS: f32 = 0.055
const LIFTS: u32 = 480
/// One cent as a share of a string's length.
const CENT: f32 = 0.000577623
/// A string whose pickup has given under `STILL` for this many samples is still.
const STILL: f32 = 1e-7
const STILL_FOR: u32 = 4096

/// What a one-pole low-pass of coefficient `c` keeps of a tone at `hz`, and the samples it
/// holds that tone back.
@audio
fn pole_keeps(c: f32, hz: f32) -> f32 {
    let w = TAU * hz / RATE
    (1.0 - c) / sqrt(1.0 - 2.0 * c * cos(w) + c * c)
}

@audio
fn pole_delay(c: f32, hz: f32) -> f32 {
    let w = TAU * hz / RATE
    atan(c * sin(w) / (1.0 - c * cos(w))) / w
}

/// The coefficient of the one-pole low-pass in the loop of a string at `hz` whose sound near
/// `top_hz` falls 60 dB in `t60` seconds: each time round, the low-pass keeps of that tone
/// what a fall that fast leaves it.
@audio
fn pole_for(hz: f32, top_hz: f32, t60: f32) -> f32 {
    let keeps = clamp(exp(-6.9078 / (hz * max(t60, 0.002))), 0.05, 0.99999)
    let w = TAU * min(max(top_hz, 2.0 * hz), 0.4 * RATE) / RATE
    let m = keeps * keeps
    let b = (1.0 - m * cos(w)) / (1.0 - m)
    clamp(1.0 / (b + sqrt(max(b * b - 1.0, 0.0))), 0.0, 0.995)
}

/// The samples an all-pass filter of coefficient `a` holds back a tone `w` radians a sample.
/// With `a` under 0 it holds low tones back longer than high ones.
@audio
fn stiff_delay(a: f32, w: f32) -> f32 {
    1.0 - 2.0 * atan(a * sin(w) / (1.0 + a * cos(w))) / w
}

/// A stiff string's harmonics aren't quite harmonics: its `n`th partial is at `n` times the
/// note times the root of `1 + b n²` (Fletcher and Rossing, The Physics of Musical
/// Instruments, 2nd ed., 1998, ch. 2), because a stiff string carries high tones faster. In
/// the loop that is `STIFFS` all-pass filters, which hold high tones back less than low ones
/// (Van Duyne and Smith, "A simplified approach to modeling dispersion caused by stiffness in
/// strings and plates", ICMC 1994). This is their coefficient for a string at `hz` whose
/// stiffness is `b`: the one that puts the partial nearest 500 Hz (the fourth or higher, and
/// under 6 kHz) where the stiffness puts it, found by halving 20 times. The partials over it
/// then come out near where they should, since what such a filter holds a tone back falls as
/// the tone's square, as a stiff string's does. (Set by the partial nearest 1.2 kHz instead,
/// a low E's partials under it were twice as sharp as they should be.)
@audio
fn stiff_for(hz: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {
    let w = TAU * hz / RATE
    let n = max(min(max(floor(500.0 / hz + 0.5), 4.0), floor(6000.0 / hz)), 2.0)
    let sooner = RATE / hz * (1.0 - sqrt((1.0 + b) / (1.0 + b * n * n)))
    var lo = -0.97
    var hi = 0.0
    for _ in 0..20 {
        let a = 0.5 * (lo + hi)
        if f32(STIFFS) * (stiff_delay(a, w) - stiff_delay(a, n * w)) > sooner {
            lo = a
        } else {
            hi = a
        }
    }
    0.5 * (lo + hi)
}

/// A smooth step: 0 at 0 and under, 1 at 1 and over.
@audio
fn rise(u: f32) -> f32 {
    if u <= 0.0 {
        return 0.0
    }
    if u >= 1.0 {
        return 1.0
    }
    0.5 - 0.5 * cos(PI * u)
}

/// One string.
struct Wire: Copy {
    /// Whether it moves; whether a finger holds its note, and the sample that note ends; and
    /// whether the note is the open string's.
    on: bool = false,
    held: bool = false,
    ends: u32 = 0,
    open: bool = true,
    /// Its note's pitch (Hz), and the samples since the note began.
    hz: f32 = 110.0,
    since: u32 = 0,
    /// The loop: the samples back it is read, the length it is going to and the share of the
    /// way it goes each sample; what comes back each time round in each plane; the low-pass it
    /// comes back through; and the coefficient of the filters that make it stiff.
    length: f32 = 400.0,
    want: f32 = 400.0,
    glide: f32 = 1.0,
    gain: [f32; 2] = [0.99; 2],
    pole: f32 = 0.0,
    stiff: f32 = 0.0,
    /// A hand on it: the samples its wave is smoothed over each time round (1: no hand).
    span: u32 = 1,
    /// What each plane's loop holds: its low-pass, the hand's sum, and the stiffness filters'
    /// last samples in and out (plane `p`'s from `p * STIFFS`).
    low: [f32; 2] = [0.0; 2],
    sum: [f32; 2] = [0.0; 2],
    went: [f32; 8] = [0.0; 8],
    came: [f32; 8] = [0.0; 8],
    /// The stroke now letting go: its swing (signed: the way the pick went), the share of it
    /// in each plane, its width and its edge (samples), the samples of it done and in all, and
    /// how loud the pick's scrape is.
    swing: f32 = 0.0,
    tilt: [f32; 2] = [1.0, 0.0],
    wide: f32 = 1.0,
    edge: f32 = 1.0,
    done: u32 = 0,
    whole: u32 = 0,
    scrape: f32 = 0.0,
    /// The way the last stroke went.
    way: f32 = -1.0,
    /// The samples left for which the pick stops what was ringing.
    hush: u32 = 0,
    /// The cents it is sharp from its stroke, falling; the cents its note is off, as the hand
    /// landed; the cents it is swayed, and the seconds before the sway starts.
    sharp: f32 = 0.0,
    off: f32 = 0.0,
    sway: f32 = 0.0,
    sway_after: f32 = 0.22,
    /// Where its sway is in its turn (0 to 1).
    swaying: f32 = 0.0,
    /// Its finger's slow drift and its quick tremble (each about 1 RMS), and the cents the
    /// finger had moved the pitch a sample ago.
    drift: f32 = 0.0,
    tremble: f32 = 0.0,
    moved: f32 = 0.0,
    /// A bend on its way: the cents the pitch was from the note's when the bend began, and
    /// the samples of it done and in all.
    bend: f32 = 0.0,
    bent: u32 = 0,
    bends: u32 = 0,
    /// A finger's noise on it: how loud, and the samples of it done and in all.
    rub: f32 = 0.0,
    rubbed: u32 = 0,
    rubs: u32 = 0,
    /// The samples its pickup has been still.
    quiet: u32 = 0,
}

/// A guitar being played: what the audio thread owns.
pub struct Picked {
    g: Guitar,
    notes: Vec<Picking>,
    next: u32,
    sample: u32,
    /// The open strings' pitches (Hz).
    open: [f32; 6],
    wires: [Wire; 6],
    /// Plane `p` of string `s` has its line from `(2 s + p) * SPAN`; the sample being written.
    lines: [f32; 24576],
    at: u32,
    /// What came back round each plane of each string over the last `PALM` samples, from
    /// `(2 s + p) * PALM`: what a hand on it smooths.
    palm: [f32; 3072],
    /// How far back each string is read for the pickup's two places (samples): the far place's
    /// first pass is the sample just written.
    far: [f32; 6],
    near_first: [f32; 6],
    near_second: [f32; 6],
    /// The pickup's resonance, the share of a stroke's sharpness kept each sample, and how
    /// much of a turn a sway makes each sample at its full rate (it wanders).
    coil: Band,
    coil_state: Svf,
    settle: f32,
    turn: f32,
    /// The hand: what makes each stroke and each note its own, its slow wanders (the sway's
    /// depth and the sway's rate), and the pick's scrape. And the cents each string is out
    /// of tune.
    hand: Noise,
    loose: [f32; 2],
    scrape: Noise,
    tuned: [f32; 6],
    /// The hand's tremble (about 1 RMS).
    shake: f32,
    amp: Amped,
    /// When its last note ends, and the samples since any string moved.
    last: u32,
    idle: u32,
}

/// A guitar, `g`, that plays `notes` (in the order they start), from its first sample. `seat`
/// is which player it is: another seat's hand wanders another way.
pub fn picked(notes: take Vec<Picking>, g: Guitar, seat: u32 = 0) -> Picked {
    assert(
        g.pickup_at > 0.5 * g.pickup_width && g.pickup_width >= 0.0 && g.pick_at > 0.0,
        "the pickup and the pick are along the string from the bridge",
    )
    var open = [0.0; 6]
    var far = [0.0; 6]
    var near_first = [0.0; 6]
    var near_second = [0.0; 6]
    for s in 0..STRINGS {
        open[s] = key_hz(g.open[s])
        assert(RATE / open[s] < f32(SPAN - 8), "an open string is no lower than 24 Hz")
        // A wave passes a place a share `x` along the string from the bridge, and again `x`
        // of a period later, upside down.
        let period = RATE / open[s]
        let a = (g.pickup_at + 0.5 * g.pickup_width) * period
        let b = (g.pickup_at - 0.5 * g.pickup_width) * period
        far[s] = min(a, f32(SPAN - 4))
        near_first[s] = 0.5 * (a - b)
        near_second[s] = min(0.5 * (a + b), f32(SPAN - 4))
    }
    var last = 0
    for n in notes {
        assert(n.string < STRINGS, "a guitar's strings are 0 to 5")
        last = max(last, n.to)
    }
    var hand = seeded(g.amp.cone + 7919 * (seat + 1))
    var tuned = [0.0; 6]
    for s in 0..STRINGS {
        tuned[s] = g.loose * TUNED * hand.next()
    }
    Picked {
        g,
        notes,
        next: 0,
        sample: 0,
        open,
        wires: [Wire {}; 6],
        lines: [0.0; 24576],
        at: 0,
        palm: [0.0; 3072],
        far,
        near_first,
        near_second,
        coil: band(hz: g.pickup_hz, q: g.pickup_q),
        coil_state: Svf {},
        settle: exp(-1.0 / (max(g.settles, 0.01) * RATE)),
        turn: 0.0,
        hand,
        loose: [0.0; 2],
        scrape: seeded(g.amp.cone + 104729 * (seat + 1)),
        tuned,
        shake: 0.0,
        amp: amped(g.amp),
        last,
        idle: 0,
    }
}

impl Picked {
    /// How long it plays, in seconds: to its last note's end.
    pub fn seconds(self) -> f32 {
        f32(self.last) / RATE
    }

    /// How long a note at `key` rings (seconds to fall 60 dB).
    @audio
    fn ring(self, key: f32) -> f32 {
        let g = self.g
        g.ring_low * pow(g.ring_high / g.ring_low, clamp((key - 40.0) / 48.0, 0.0, 1.0))
    }

    /// The sample `back` samples ago in the line that starts at `base` (a fraction of a sample
    /// by the two either side).
    @audio
    fn read(self, base: u32, back: f32) -> f32 {
        let whole = u32(back)
        let part = back - f32(whole)
        let i = base + ((self.at + SPAN - whole) & (SPAN - 1))
        let j = base + ((self.at + SPAN - whole - 1) & (SPAN - 1))
        self.lines[i] + part * (self.lines[j] - self.lines[i])
    }

    /// Sets string `s`'s loop for a note at `hz` that falls 60 dB in `ring` seconds, with a
    /// hand on the string or not: what comes back each time round, through what, and the
    /// length that makes the loop one period long with what its filters hold the wave back.
    @audio
    fn tune(mut self, s: u32, hz: f32, ring: f32, hand: bool, press: f32 = 1.0) {
        let g = self.g
        let period = RATE / hz
        // A stopped note's top goes to the fingertip.
        let stopped = hz > 1.03 * self.open[s]
        let top = if stopped { min(g.shine, g.finger * 110.0 / hz) } else { g.shine }
        let pole = pole_for(hz, SHINE_HZ, top)
        // A string stopped shorter is stiffer for its length.
        let ratio = hz / self.open[s]
        let stiff = stiff_for(hz, g.stiff[s] * ratio * ratio)
        var span = 1
        var smoothed = 1.0
        if hand {
            let low_e = press * g.mute_over * RATE / LOW_E * pow(LOW_E / hz, 1.5)
            span = clamp(u32(low_e), 2, max(min(PALM - 1, u32(0.45 * period)), 2))
            // What a mean over `span` samples keeps of the note itself.
            let half = PI * hz / RATE
            smoothed = abs(sin(half * f32(span)) / (f32(span) * sin(half)))
            for i in 0..PLANES * PALM {
                self.palm[2 * s * PALM + i] = 0.0
            }
        }
        let lost = pole_keeps(pole, hz) * smoothed
        self.wires[s].hz = hz
        self.wires[s].pole = pole
        self.wires[s].stiff = stiff
        self.wires[s].span = span
        self.wires[s].sum = [0.0; 2]
        // Across the pickup the string gives more to the bridge, and rings four fifths as long.
        self.wires[s].gain = [
            min(exp(-6.9078 / (hz * max(ring, 0.002))) / lost, 0.99995),
            min(exp(-6.9078 / (hz * max(0.8 * ring, 0.002))) / lost, 0.99995),
        ]
        self.wires[s].want = clamp(
            period -
                pole_delay(pole, hz) -
                0.5 * f32(span - 1) -
                f32(STIFFS) * stiff_delay(stiff, TAU * hz / RATE),
            4.0,
            f32(SPAN - 4),
        )
    }

    /// Starts note `n`.
    @audio
    fn begin(mut self, n: Picking) {
        let g = self.g
        let s = n.string
        let hz = key_hz(n.key)
        let was_on = self.wires[s].on
        let was_hz = self.wires[s].hz
        if n.muted {
            // The heel never presses twice alike.
            let press = 1.0 + 0.25 * self.hand.next()
            self.tune(s, hz, ring: g.mute, hand: true, press: press)
        } else {
            self.tune(s, hz, ring: self.ring(n.key), hand: false)
        }
        var w = self.wires[s]
        let v = clamp(n.hard, 0.0, 1.0)
        // The stroke's width: the pick's place, a little off the usual.
        let place = g.pick_at * (1.0 + PLACES * self.hand.next())
        let wide = max(min(place * RATE / self.open[s], 0.5 * RATE / hz), 1.0)
        if n.picked || !was_on {
            // The pick: it stops what was ringing for one trip round the string, and lets
            // the string go at its new length, scraping as it leaves.
            if was_on {
                w.hush = u32(w.length) + 2
            } else {
                w.low = [0.0; 2]
                w.went = [0.0; 8]
                w.came = [0.0; 8]
                w.hush = 0
            }
            w.length = w.want
            w.glide = 1.0
            w.way = -w.way
            w.swing = w.way * PICK * pow(v, g.touch)
            let angle = TILT + TILTS * self.hand.next()
            w.tilt = [cos(angle), sin(angle)]
            w.edge = max((g.pick_soft + (g.pick_hard - g.pick_soft) * v) * 0.001 * RATE, 1.0)
            w.wide = wide
            w.done = 0
            w.whole = max(u32(w.wide + w.edge) + 1, SCRAPES)
            w.scrape = g.scrape * PICK * pow(v, g.touch)
            w.sharp = g.sharp * v * v
            w.off = LANDS * (0.5 + 0.5 * self.hand.next())
            w.bends = 0
            if n.scoop > 0.0 {
                // Slid into: picked a fret or two under, the finger already on its way up,
                // rubbing the string as it goes.
                w.bend = -100.0 * n.scoop
                w.bent = 0
                w.bends = max(u32(SCOOPS * (1.0 + 0.25 * self.hand.next()) * RATE), 1)
                w.rub = g.fingers * PICK * (if s < 3 { 2.0 } else { 0.6 })
                w.rubbed = 0
                w.rubs = w.bends
            }
        } else {
            // No pick. A bend: the string is at its new length at once, and its pitch is
            // carried from where it was to the note's as a finger pushes a string, slowly at
            // first, quickest in the middle, settling at the end (never in quite the same
            // time, nor to quite the same place). Or a finger hammers on or pulls off: the
            // length changes at once, and the finger adds a little swing.
            if n.glide > 0.0 {
                let time = n.glide * (1.0 + 0.2 * self.hand.next())
                var still_to_go = 0.0
                if w.bent < w.bends {
                    still_to_go = w.bend * (1.0 - rise(f32(w.bent) / f32(w.bends)))
                }
                w.bend = 1200.0 * log2(was_hz / hz) + still_to_go
                w.bent = 0
                w.bends = max(u32(time * RATE), 1)
                w.length = w.want
                w.glide = 1.0
                w.off = BEND_LANDS * self.hand.next()
            } else {
                w.glide = SNAP
                w.swing = w.way * TAP * PICK * pow(v, g.touch)
                w.edge = max(g.pick_soft * 0.001 * RATE, 1.0)
                w.wide = wide
                w.done = 0
                w.whole = u32(w.wide + w.edge) + 1
                w.scrape = 0.0
                w.off = LANDS * (0.5 + 0.5 * self.hand.next())
                w.bends = 0
            }
        }
        w.on = true
        w.held = true
        w.ends = n.to
        w.since = 0
        w.open = abs(n.key - g.open[s]) < 0.5
        w.swaying = 0.0
        w.sway = n.sway * (1.0 + SWAY_OWN * self.hand.next())
        w.sway_after = SWAY_AFTER * (1.0 + SWAY_OWN * self.hand.next())
        w.quiet = 0
        self.wires[s] = w
    }

    /// The next sample of string `s`, as the pickup hears it.
    @audio
    fn string(mut self, s: u32) -> f32 {
        let g = self.g
        var w = self.wires[s]
        // A finger lets go: the string is stopped.
        if w.held && self.sample >= w.ends {
            self.tune(s, w.hz, ring: g.stop, hand: true)
            let stopped = self.wires[s]
            w.pole = stopped.pole
            w.stiff = stopped.stiff
            w.span = stopped.span
            w.sum = [0.0; 2]
            w.gain = stopped.gain
            w.want = stopped.want
            w.glide = SNAP
            w.held = false
            w.sway = 0.0
            if !w.open {
                w.rub = 0.5 * g.fingers * PICK
                w.rubbed = 0
                w.rubs = LIFTS
            }
        }
        // The pitch: the length on its way, the stroke's sharpness falling, and what a finger
        // on the string adds: where it landed, its drift, and a sway.
        w.length += w.glide * (w.want - w.length)
        var cents = w.sharp + self.tuned[s]
        w.sharp = w.sharp * self.settle
        w.since += 1
        if !w.open {
            // The finger's press wanders: noise through a low-pass at 1 Hz, about 1 RMS.
            let slow = TAU / RATE
            w.drift += slow * (self.hand.next() / (0.57735 * sqrt(0.5 * slow)) - w.drift)
            // The hand's tremble, and this finger's own.
            let quick = TAU * TREMBLE_HZ / RATE
            w.tremble += quick * (self.hand.next() / (0.57735 * sqrt(0.5 * quick)) - w.tremble)
            let trembles = TREMBLES * self.shake + TREMBLE_OWN * w.tremble
            cents += g.loose * (w.off + DRIFTS * w.drift + trembles)
            let held = f32(w.since) / RATE
            if w.sway > 0.0 && held > w.sway_after {
                let swayed = held - w.sway_after
                let depth = w.sway *
                    min(swayed / SWAY_IN, 1.0) *
                    (1.0 + SWAY_GROWS * min(swayed, 2.0))
                let loose = max(1.0 + SWAY_LOOSE * self.loose[0], 0.0)
                cents += depth * loose * (0.5 - 0.5 * cos(TAU * w.swaying))
                // Each note's sway starts from the note's own pitch, slowly.
                let quickened = SWAY_SLOW + (1.0 - SWAY_SLOW) * min(swayed / SWAY_QUICKENS, 1.0)
                w.swaying = fract(w.swaying + quickened * self.turn)
            }
        }
        // A bend is far enough that a cent isn't a fixed share of the length.
        var longer = 1.0 - CENT * cents
        let bending = w.bent < w.bends
        if bending {
            cents += w.bend * (1.0 - rise(f32(w.bent) / f32(w.bends)))
            w.bent += 1
            longer = exp2(-cents / 1200.0)
        }
        let back = clamp(w.length * longer, 4.0, f32(SPAN - 4))
        // How fast the finger is moving the pitch: a string pushed across its fret rubs.
        let by_finger = cents - w.sharp - self.tuned[s]
        let rubbing = min(abs(by_finger - w.moved) / RUB_AT, 2.0)
        w.moved = by_finger
        // The stroke: the string's speed where it was let go is a pulse, with the pick's
        // scrape over its start.
        var stroke = 0.0
        if w.done < w.whole {
            let i = f32(w.done)
            stroke = w.swing * (rise((i + 1.0) / w.edge) - rise((i + 1.0 - w.wide) / w.edge))
            if w.done < SCRAPES && w.scrape > 0.0 {
                let through = (i + 0.5) / f32(SCRAPES)
                stroke += w.scrape * sin(PI * through) * sin(PI * through) * self.scrape.next()
            }
            w.done += 1
        }
        // A finger's noise on the string: the rub of a string moved across its fret, and the
        // scratch of a finger leaving or sliding.
        if bending && !w.open && w.held {
            let wound = if s < 3 { 2.0 } else { 0.4 }
            stroke += RUBS * wound * g.fingers * PICK * rubbing * self.scrape.next()
        }
        if w.rubbed < w.rubs {
            let through = (f32(w.rubbed) + 0.5) / f32(w.rubs)
            stroke += w.rub * sin(PI * through) * sin(PI * through) * self.scrape.next()
            w.rubbed += 1
        }
        let hushed = w.hush > 0
        if hushed {
            w.hush -= 1
        }
        // A stopped string's slipping: this sample's.
        var slips = 0.0
        if !w.open && w.held {
            slips = (if s < 3 { 2.5 } else { 1.0 }) * g.sizzle * self.scrape.next()
        }
        var heard = 0.0
        for p in 0..PLANES {
            let base = (2 * s + p) * SPAN
            // The plane across the pickup is a little sharp of the one toward it.
            let far_back = if p == 0 { back } else { back * (1.0 - CENT * g.planes) }
            // The wave as it was that many samples ago: by the four samples round that moment
            // (a cubic through them: Laakso, Välimäki, Karjalainen and Laine, "Splitting the
            // unit delay", IEEE Signal Processing Magazine 13(1), 1996), which keeps the top
            // that the two nearest alone would lose a little of each time round.
            let whole = u32(far_back)
            let d = far_back - f32(whole)
            let from = self.at + SPAN - whole
            let x0 = self.lines[base + ((from + 1) & (SPAN - 1))]
            let x1 = self.lines[base + (from & (SPAN - 1))]
            let x2 = self.lines[base + ((from - 1) & (SPAN - 1))]
            let x3 = self.lines[base + ((from - 2) & (SPAN - 1))]
            var round = -d * (d - 1.0) * (d - 2.0) / 6.0 * x0 +
                (d + 1.0) * (d - 1.0) * (d - 2.0) / 2.0 * x1 -
                (d + 1.0) * d * (d - 2.0) / 2.0 * x2 +
                (d + 1.0) * d * (d - 1.0) / 6.0 * x3
            // What the trip round took: a hand on the string, then the string's stiffness,
            // then its own top.
            if w.span > 1 {
                let k = (2 * s + p) * PALM
                w.sum[p] += round - self.palm[k + ((self.at + PALM - w.span) & (PALM - 1))]
                self.palm[k + (self.at & (PALM - 1))] = round
                round = w.sum[p] / f32(w.span)
            }
            for k in 0..STIFFS {
                let i = p * STIFFS + k
                let out = w.stiff * (round - w.came[i]) + w.went[i]
                w.went[i] = round
                w.came[i] = out
                round = out
            }
            w.low[p] += (1.0 - w.pole) * (round - w.low[p])
            var y = w.gain[p] * w.low[p]
            if hushed {
                y = HUSH * y
            }
            y += w.tilt[p] * stroke + slips * abs(y)
            self.lines[base + self.at] = y
            // The pickup's two places: at each the wave passes, and passes again upside
            // down. It hears the plane across it less.
            let passes = 0.5 *
                (y -
                    self.read(base, self.far[s]) +
                    self.read(base, self.near_first[s]) -
                    self.read(base, self.near_second[s]))
            heard += if p == 0 { passes } else { g.across * passes }
        }
        if abs(heard) < STILL && w.done >= w.whole && w.rubbed >= w.rubs {
            w.quiet += 1
        } else {
            w.quiet = 0
        }
        self.wires[s] = w
        if w.quiet > STILL_FOR {
            self.wires[s].on = false
            self.wires[s].held = false
            for i in 0..PLANES * SPAN {
                self.lines[2 * s * SPAN + i] = 0.0
            }
        }
        heard
    }

    /// The next sample of what the pickup gives the amplifier.
    @audio
    fn pickup(mut self) -> f32 {
        let t = self.sample
        loop {
            if self.next >= self.notes.len() || self.notes[self.next].from > t {
                break
            }
            self.begin(self.notes[self.next])
            self.next += 1
        }
        // The hand's wanders: noise through one-pole low-passes at 1 Hz, scaled to about 1 RMS
        // (as engine::bowed's are).
        let slow = TAU / RATE
        let scale = 1.0 / (0.57735 * sqrt(0.5 * slow))
        for k in 0..2 {
            self.loose[k] += slow * (scale * self.hand.next() - self.loose[k])
        }
        let quick = TAU * TREMBLE_HZ / RATE
        self.shake += quick * (self.hand.next() / (0.57735 * sqrt(0.5 * quick)) - self.shake)
        var heard = 0.0
        var moving = false
        for s in 0..STRINGS {
            if self.wires[s].on {
                heard += self.string(s)
                moving = true
            }
        }
        self.at = (self.at + 1) & (SPAN - 1)
        self.turn = SWAY_HZ * (1.0 + 0.25 * SWAY_LOOSE * self.loose[1]) / RATE
        self.idle = if moving { 0 } else { self.idle + 1 }
        self.sample += 1
        self.coil_state.low(self.coil, heard)
    }

    /// The next sample.
    @audio
    fn one(mut self) -> f32 {
        let coil = self.pickup()
        self.amp.next(coil)
    }

    /// The next samples, into `out`.
    @audio
    pub fn play(mut self, out: mut [f32]) {
        for n in 0..out.len() {
            out[n] = self.one()
        }
    }

    /// Whether nothing of it sounds, nor will: its notes are done, and no string has moved
    /// for half a second.
    @audio
    pub fn silent(self) -> bool {
        self.next >= self.notes.len() && self.idle > 24000
    }

    /// Adds its next samples to samples `from` to `to` of `left` and `right`, the same to both.
    /// Its notes' times count from its first sample, so it is sounded from a piece's start
    /// without a gap.
    @audio
    pub fn sound(mut self, left: mut [f32], right: mut [f32], from: u32, to: u32) {
        for n in from..to {
            let y = self.one()
            left[n] += y
            right[n] += y
        }
    }
}

// The tests render on the program's thread, as the audio thread would.

/// `quanta` quanta of `p`, sample by sample.
fn rendered(p: mut Picked, quanta: u32) -> Vec<f32> {
    var out: Vec<f32> = Vec::with_capacity(128 * quanta)
    var quantum = [0.0; 128]
    for _ in 0..quanta {
        p.play(mut quantum)
        for i in 0..128 {
            out.push(quantum[i])
        }
    }
    out
}

/// `count` samples of what `p`'s pickup gives, before the amplifier and the speaker (whose
/// own resonances ring a moment at their own pitches).
fn picked_up(p: mut Picked, count: u32) -> Vec<f32> {
    var out: Vec<f32> = Vec::with_capacity(count)
    for _ in 0..count {
        out.push(p.pickup())
    }
    out
}

/// The mean square of the `width` samples of `x` from `at`.
fn power(x: [f32], at: u32, width: u32) -> f32 {
    var sum = 0.0
    for i in at..at + width {
        sum += x[i] * x[i]
    }
    sum / f32(width)
}

/// The pitch (Hz) of the `width` samples of `x` from `at`, sought within 3% of `near`: the lag
/// at which it repeats best, between samples by a parabola.
fn pitch_of(x: [f32], at: u32, width: u32, near: f32) -> f32 {
    let lo = u32(RATE / (1.03 * near))
    let hi = u32(RATE / (0.97 * near)) + 1
    var best = lo
    var best_score = -2.0
    var scores: Vec<f32> = Vec::new()
    for lag in lo - 1..hi + 2 {
        var both = 0.0
        var a = 0.0
        var b = 0.0
        for i in at..at + width {
            both += x[i] * x[i + lag]
            a += x[i] * x[i]
            b += x[i + lag] * x[i + lag]
        }
        let score = both / sqrt(max(a * b, 1e-30))
        scores.push(score)
        if lag >= lo && lag <= hi && score > best_score {
            best_score = score
            best = lag
        }
    }
    let k = best - (lo - 1)
    let (before, here, after) = (scores[k - 1], scores[k], scores[k + 1])
    let bend = before - 2.0 * here + after
    let shift = if bend < -1e-9 { 0.5 * (before - after) / bend } else { 0.0 }
    RATE / (f32(best) + clamp(shift, -0.5, 0.5))
}

/// How far `hz` is from `asked`, in cents.
fn cents(hz: f32, asked: f32) -> f32 {
    1200.0 * log2(hz / asked)
}

/// One note on `g`, on the string it lies lowest on.
fn one_note(g: Guitar, key: f32, to: u32, muted: bool = false) -> Picked {
    picked(Vec::from([Picking { from: 0, to, key, string: string_for(g, key), muted }]), g)
}

/// A guitar with nothing loose in it: strings that aren't stiff and move one way, exactly in
/// tune, under fingers that land where they are asked, and a pick that doesn't stretch them.
fn exact(g: Guitar) -> Guitar {
    Guitar { sharp: 0.0, loose: 0.0, sizzle: 0.0, stiff: [0.0; 6], planes: 0.0, ..g }
}

/// What a loop's filters hold a tone back, the loop is that much shorter to make up for: an
/// open low E, notes up the neck and at the top of the fingerboard are within 3 cents of what
/// is asked, and so is a palm-muted low E, whose wave the hand smooths over a tenth of its
/// period each time round.
@test
fn each_note_sounds_as_asked_ringing_or_muted() {
    let plain = exact(CLEAN)
    for key in [40.0, 57.0, 76.0, 86.0] {
        var p = one_note(plain, key, to: 48000)
        let x = picked_up(mut p, count: 25600)
        let hz = pitch_of(x, at: 9600, width: 7200, near: key_hz(key))
        let off = cents(hz, key_hz(key))
        assert(abs(off) < 3.0, f"key {key} sounds {off} cents from itself")
    }
    var m = one_note(plain, 40.0, to: 48000, muted: true)
    let x = picked_up(mut m, count: 7680)
    let off = cents(pitch_of(x, at: 1200, width: 3600, near: key_hz(40.0)), key_hz(40.0))
    assert(abs(off) < 3.0, f"a muted low E sounds {off} cents from itself")
}

/// A note held rings on; a finger let go stops its string within a fifth of a second; and a
/// palm-muted note has all but died in the time a ringing one has hardly fallen.
@test
fn a_held_note_rings_a_stopped_one_dies_and_a_palm_mutes() {
    var held = one_note(CLEAN, 45.0, to: 96000)
    let h = rendered(mut held, quanta: 500)
    let early = power(h, at: 4800, width: 4800)
    let late = power(h, at: 52800, width: 4800)
    assert(late > 0.1 * early, f"held a second, a note has {late / early} of its power")
    var let_go = one_note(CLEAN, 45.0, to: 24000)
    let l = rendered(mut let_go, quanta: 500)
    let after = power(l, at: 33600, width: 4800)
    assert(after < 1e-4 * early, f"a fifth of a second after its finger, {after / early}")
    assert(let_go.silent(), "a stopped string never came to rest")
    var muted = one_note(CLEAN, 45.0, to: 96000, muted: true)
    let m = rendered(mut muted, quanta: 200)
    let thud = power(m, at: 0, width: 4800)
    let gone = power(m, at: 19200, width: 4800)
    assert(thud > 0.05 * early, f"a muted note's start has {thud / early} of a ringing one's")
    assert(gone < 0.02 * thud, f"0.4 s on, a muted note has {gone / thud} of its start")
}

/// How strong the tone at `hz` is over the `width` samples of `x` from `at` (dB).
fn tone_db(x: [f32], at: u32, width: u32, hz: f32) -> f32 {
    var re = 0.0
    var im = 0.0
    for i in 0..width {
        let turn = TAU * fract(hz * f32(i) / RATE)
        re += x[at + i] * cos(turn)
        im += x[at + i] * sin(turn)
    }
    8.685889 * log(2.0 * sqrt(re * re + im * im) / f32(width) + 1e-12)
}

/// The strongest tone under 6 kHz in `x` (a fifth of a second from `at`) that a harmonic of
/// `hz` over the audio rate's half would fold back to, and isn't itself a harmonic: in dB
/// beside the note.
fn strays(x: [f32], at: u32, hz: u32) -> f32 {
    let note = tone_db(x, at, width: 9600, hz: f32(hz))
    var worst = -200.0
    for k in 2..160 {
        let f = (k * hz) % 48000
        let folded = if f > 24000 { 48000 - f } else { f }
        if k * hz > 24000 && folded > 40 && folded < 6000 && folded % hz != 0 {
            worst = max(worst, tone_db(x, at, width: 9600, hz: f32(folded)) - note)
        }
    }
    worst
}

/// Clipping a tone makes harmonics past half the audio rate, which 48,000 samples a second
/// can't hold: they come back as tones that are no harmonic of the note. A 1245 Hz tone
/// through `LEAD_AMP`'s valves, with the valves run at the audio rate (and their corners set
/// for it), has such a tone 34.2 dB under the note; run at four times the rate, between
/// half-band filters, the strongest is 76.0 dB under. (At 660 Hz: 43.7 and 97.9; at 415 Hz:
/// 57.7 and 100.4.)
@test
fn the_valves_leave_no_tones_that_arent_the_notes() {
    var a = amped(LEAD.amp)
    var x: Vec<f32> = Vec::with_capacity(24000)
    for i in 0..24000 {
        x.push(a.driven(0.5 * sin(TAU * fract(1245.0 * f32(i) / RATE))))
    }
    let worst = strays(x, at: 9600, hz: 1245)
    assert(worst < -65.0, f"the strongest stray tone is {worst} dB beside the note")
}

/// A score's one voice, played exactly at 120 quarter notes a minute.
fn deadpan_picks(lines: [Text], g: Guitar, hand: Hand) -> Vec<Picking> {
    let s = score::score("test", score::meter(beats: 4, unit: 4)).voice("guitar", lines)
    pickings(s, perform::deadpan(s, tempo: 120.0), 0, g, hand)
}

/// A chord's notes each take a string, and the strum crosses them a moment apart: from the
/// lowest string on the beat; off it from the highest, softer, and only four strings far.
@test
fn a_chord_is_strummed_down_on_the_beat_and_up_off_it() {
    let picks = deadpan_picks(
        ["<e2 b2 e3 g#3 b3 e4>/8 <e2 b2 e3 g#3 b3 e4>/8 r/4 r/2 |"],
        CLEAN,
        Hand { uneven: 0.0 },
    )
    assert(picks.len() == 10, f"a down strum of six and an up strum of four are {picks.len()}")
    // An open E chord lies on all six strings, lowest key on the lowest.
    for i in 0..6 {
        assert(picks[i].string == i, f"the down strum's note {i} is on string {picks[i].string}")
        assert(picks[i].from == i * 528, f"the down strum's note {i} starts at {picks[i].from}")
    }
    for i in 0..4 {
        let n = picks[6 + i]
        assert(n.string == 5 - i, f"the up strum's note {i} is on string {n.string}")
        assert(n.from == 12000 + i * 528, f"the up strum's note {i} starts at {n.from}")
        assert(n.hard < picks[0].hard, "an up strum is softer")
    }
}

/// A slurred note isn't picked: with a hand that bends, a step up is bent to, and the pitch
/// arrives; a wider slur is hammered on. A note marked staccato is palm-muted.
@test
fn a_slur_is_bent_or_hammered_and_a_staccato_is_muted() {
    let picks = deadpan_picks(["a4/2 ( b4/4 e5/8 ) e3/8 !staccato |"], LEAD, Hand { bends: 2 })
    assert(picks[0].picked && !picks[1].picked && !picks[2].picked && picks[3].picked)
    assert(picks[1].string == picks[0].string && picks[1].glide > 0.0, "a step up is bent to")
    assert(picks[2].glide == 0.0, "a fourth up is hammered on")
    assert(picks[3].muted && !picks[0].muted, "the staccato note alone is muted")
    // The bent note, a quarter of a second after it starts (the second note is at 1 s).
    var p = picked(picks.clone(), exact(CLEAN))
    let x = picked_up(mut p, count: 66560)
    let off = cents(pitch_of(x, at: 60000, width: 4800, near: key_hz(71.0)), key_hz(71.0))
    assert(abs(off) < 6.0, f"the bend arrives {off} cents from its note")
    let before = cents(pitch_of(x, at: 38400, width: 4800, near: key_hz(69.0)), key_hz(69.0))
    assert(abs(before) < 4.0, f"the note before the bend is {before} cents from itself")
    // A finger pushes slowly at first: 20 to 40 ms into a bend of a sixth of a second the
    // pitch has gone a tenth of the way. (A length going a share of the way each sample, a
    // synthesizer's glide, had gone a third of the way: 70 cents.)
    let near = key_hz(69.0) * exp2(15.0 / 1200.0)
    let early = cents(pitch_of(x, at: 48960, width: 960, near: near), key_hz(69.0))
    assert(early > 2.0 && early < 40.0, f"20 to 40 ms into the bend the pitch is {early} cents up")
}

/// The frequency (Hz) of the partial of `x` nearest `near`, sought from 8 cents under it to 30
/// over, a cent at a time, over the `width` samples from `at`.
fn partial_hz(x: [f32], at: u32, width: u32, near: f32) -> f32 {
    var best = near
    var most = -400.0
    for c in 0..39 {
        let hz = near * exp2((f32(c) - 8.0) / 1200.0)
        let db = tone_db(x, at, width, hz)
        if db > most {
            most = db
            best = hz
        }
    }
    best
}

/// A stiff string's partials are sharp of harmonics, the more the higher: a low E's tenth is
/// 11 cents sharp of ten times the note, and the note itself is where it is asked (the loop
/// is shorter by what the stiffness filters hold the note back).
@test
fn a_stiff_strings_partials_are_as_sharp_as_its_stiffness_says() {
    let g = Guitar { sharp: 0.0, loose: 0.0, planes: 0.0, ..CLEAN }
    var p = one_note(g, 40.0, to: 48000)
    let x = picked_up(mut p, count: 28800)
    let note = key_hz(40.0)
    let first = cents(partial_hz(x, at: 4800, width: 19200, near: note), note)
    let tenth = cents(partial_hz(x, at: 4800, width: 19200, near: 10.0 * note), 10.0 * note)
    let stiff = 600.0 * log2((1.0 + g.stiff[0] * 100.0) / (1.0 + g.stiff[0]))
    assert(abs(first) < 2.0, f"a stiff low E sounds {first} cents from itself")
    assert(abs(tenth - stiff) < 3.0, f"its tenth partial is {tenth} cents sharp, not {stiff}")
}

/// How far the `n`th harmonic of a held A4 on `g` strays from a straight line through its
/// levels (RMS dB), over 30 ms frames a hundredth of a second apart from 0.15 s to 0.55 s.
fn strays_from_steady(g: Guitar, n: f32) -> f32 {
    var p = one_note(g, 69.0, to: 48000)
    let x = rendered(mut p, quanta: 230)
    var levels = [0.0; 41]
    var mean = 0.0
    for f in 0..41 {
        levels[f] = tone_db(x, at: 7200 + 480 * f, width: 1440, hz: n * key_hz(69.0))
        mean += levels[f] / 41.0
    }
    // The line of best fit through the levels.
    var rise = 0.0
    var run = 0.0
    for f in 0..41 {
        rise += (f32(f) - 20.0) * (levels[f] - mean)
        run += (f32(f) - 20.0) * (f32(f) - 20.0)
    }
    var off = 0.0
    for f in 0..41 {
        let line = mean + rise / run * (f32(f) - 20.0)
        off += (levels[f] - line) * (levels[f] - line) / 41.0
    }
    sqrt(off)
}

/// A driven note isn't a frozen wave. Valves clip a held note to one level, so what the ear
/// follows in it is how its harmonics move. From strings that aren't stiff and move one way,
/// dead in tune, the driven wave is the same every period: an A4's second and third
/// harmonics stray 0.1 dB from steady, and the lead was heard as a synthesizer. A stiff
/// string's partials drift against each other, its two ways of moving beat, and a hand
/// trembles: the same harmonics stray 0.8 dB. (The second and third because they are strong:
/// a weak harmonic's level is the valves' doing, and moves either way.)
@test
fn a_driven_note_isnt_a_frozen_wave() {
    // Both with no fingertip taking the note's top, which moves a harmonic too, one way only.
    let held = Guitar { finger: 1000.0, ..LEAD }
    let frozen = 0.5 * (strays_from_steady(exact(held), 2.0) + strays_from_steady(exact(held), 3.0))
    let alive = 0.5 * (strays_from_steady(held, 2.0) + strays_from_steady(held, 3.0))
    assert(alive > 0.5, f"a driven A4's low harmonics stray {alive} dB from steady")
    assert(
        frozen < 0.3 * alive,
        f"from an exact string they stray {frozen} dB, {alive} from a real one",
    )
}

/// The mean square of `count` samples of what `a`'s valves make of a 440 Hz tone `level` high.
fn driven_power(a: mut Amped, level: f32, count: u32) -> f32 {
    var sum = 0.0
    for i in 0..count {
        let y = a.driven(level * sin(TAU * fract(440.0 * f32(i) / RATE)))
        sum += y * y
    }
    sum / f32(count)
}

/// How loud a quiet 440 Hz tone comes through an amplifier whose valves choke `spit` much in
/// the 20 ms just after a loud one, beside 150 ms later (dB).
fn just_after_a_loud_sound(spit: f32) -> f32 {
    var a = amped(Amp { drive: 30.0, spit, sag: 0.0, hiss: -200.0 })
    var x: Vec<f32> = Vec::with_capacity(12000)
    for i in 0..12000 {
        let level = if i < 2400 { 1.0 } else { 0.01 }
        x.push(a.driven(level * sin(TAU * fract(440.0 * f32(i) / RATE))))
    }
    tone_db(x, at: 2496, width: 960, hz: 440.0) - tone_db(x, at: 9600, width: 960, hz: 440.0)
}

/// A valve that chokes: a loud sound charges its grid, and for a few hundredths of a second
/// after it a quiet sound hardly gets through (14 dB down); one that doesn't choke passes the
/// quiet sound at once (1 dB down).
@test
fn a_valve_that_chokes_sputters_after_a_loud_sound() {
    let choked = just_after_a_loud_sound(0.8)
    let free = just_after_a_loud_sound(0.0)
    assert(
        choked < -10.0,
        f"just after a loud sound a choking valve passes a quiet one {choked} dB",
    )
    assert(abs(free) < 2.0, f"a valve that doesn't choke passes it {free} dB")
}

/// A gate shuts when the strings stop, and the hiss goes with them; without one the hiss is
/// always there.
@test
fn a_gate_shuts_the_hiss_out() {
    var gated = amped(Amp { drive: 40.0, hiss: -80.0, gate: -55.0 })
    var open = amped(Amp { drive: 40.0, hiss: -80.0 })
    let _ = driven_power(mut gated, level: 0.3, count: 4800)
    let _ = driven_power(mut open, level: 0.3, count: 4800)
    // A fifth of a second for the gate to shut, then a tenth of a second of nothing played.
    let _ = driven_power(mut gated, level: 0.0, count: 9600)
    let _ = driven_power(mut open, level: 0.0, count: 9600)
    let shut = driven_power(mut gated, level: 0.0, count: 4800)
    let hiss = driven_power(mut open, level: 0.0, count: 4800)
    assert(hiss > 1e-6, f"an amplifier driven this hard hisses: {hiss}")
    assert(shut < 1e-4 * hiss, f"behind a gate the hiss is {shut}, {hiss} without")
}

/// The highs' share of what the pickup gives 0.3 to 0.4 s into an A4 on `g`: the energy of
/// its sample-to-sample differences beside its energy.
fn highs_share(g: Guitar) -> f32 {
    var p = one_note(g, 69.0, to: 48000)
    let x = picked_up(mut p, count: 19200)
    var whole = 0.0
    var highs = 0.0
    for i in 14400..19200 {
        whole += x[i] * x[i]
        highs += (x[i] - x[i - 1]) * (x[i] - x[i - 1])
    }
    highs / whole
}

/// A fingertip takes a stopped note's top: 0.3 s into an A4 on the first string, what the
/// pickup gives has far less of its highs beside the whole than the same note with no
/// fingertip in the loop.
@test
fn a_fingertip_takes_a_stopped_notes_top() {
    let stopped = highs_share(exact(CLEAN))
    let bare = highs_share(Guitar { finger: 1000.0, ..exact(CLEAN) })
    assert(stopped < 0.75 * bare, f"highs' share with a fingertip {stopped}, without {bare}")
}

/// A hand that plays a line slides into a phrase's first note from a fret under, and is late
/// leaving one string for the next, so the two notes sound together a moment.
@test
fn a_phrase_is_slid_into_and_a_finger_is_late_off_its_string() {
    let picks = deadpan_picks(["a4/4 d4/4 r/2 |"], LEAD, SOLO)
    assert(picks[0].scoop == 1.0 && picks[1].scoop == 0.0, "the first note alone is slid into")
    assert(picks[0].string != picks[1].string, "the two notes are on two strings")
    assert(
        picks[0].to > picks[1].from && picks[0].to < picks[1].from + 4800,
        f"the first note ends at {picks[0].to}, the second starts at {picks[1].from}",
    )
}
